    "problem_statement": "# tick() method in StepTickTimeFactory broken for all delta types\n\n#### MCVE Code Sample\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nfrom freezegun import freeze_time\n\n# Example 1: Using integer\nfirst_time = datetime.datetime(2020, 1, 14, 0, 0, 0)\nwith freeze_time(first_time) as frozen_time:\n    frozen_time.tick(1)  # Should increment by 1 second\n    # TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +=: 'datetime.datetime' and 'int'\n\n# Example 2: Using float\nwith freeze_time(first_time) as frozen_time:\n    frozen_time.tick(1.5)  # Should increment by 1.5 seconds\n    # TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +=: 'datetime.datetime' and 'float'\n\n# Example 3: Using timedelta\nwith freeze_time(first_time) as frozen_time:\n    frozen_time.tick(datetime.timedelta(seconds=2))  # Should increment by 2 seconds\n    # This also fails\n```\n\n#### Expected Output\nThe time should be incremented by the specified amount, and the tick() method should return the new frozen time.\n\n#### Problem Description\nI'm trying to use the tick() method to manually increment time in my tests, but it's failing for all types of delta inputs (integers, floats, and timedeltas). The method seems to be trying to add the delta directly to the datetime object without converting it to a timedelta first.\n\nThis is particularly problematic when using auto_tick_seconds together with manual ticks, as the auto-tick functionality works but manual ticks fail with TypeError.\n\n#### Versions\nfreezegun: 1.2.2\nPython: 3.10.8"
